   </dc:creator>
   <dc:title>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Multi-Objective AGV Task Allocation and Path Planning: Evaluation of Deterministic and Metaheuristic Algorithms</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Multi-Objective AGV Task Allocation and Path Planning: Evaluation of Deterministic and Metaheuristic Algorithms</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:title>
   <dc:subject>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>automated guided vehicles; path planning; scheduling; multi-objective optimization</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:subject>
   <dc:type>
    <rdf:Bag>
     <rdf:li>Text</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Bag>
   </dc:type>
   <dc:description>
    <rdf:Alt>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">Automated guided vehicles (AGVs) enable material transport in automated manufacturing facilities</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">Automated guided vehicles (AGVs) enable material transport in automated manufacturing facilities</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">where fleet efficiency depends on coordinating task allocation and route generation while balancing completion time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">where fleet efficiency depends on coordinating task allocation and route generation while balancing completion time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and travel distance. This study develops a Python(version: 3.14.6)-based multi-objective evaluation framework for offline AGV path planning and task assignment in grid-based warehouse environments with known</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and travel distance. This study develops a Python(version: 3.14.6)-based multi-objective evaluation framework for offline AGV path planning and task assignment in grid-based warehouse environments with known</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">static obstacles. Particle swarm optimization (PSO) and the gravitational search algorithm (GSA) are evaluated against deterministic A* search across warehouse scenarios with different layouts and obstacle configurations. Each planner is integrated into a common greedy assignment procedure that processes tasks according to priority and earliest-start ordering. For each task</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">static obstacles. Particle swarm optimization (PSO) and the gravitational search algorithm (GSA) are evaluated against deterministic A* search across warehouse scenarios with different layouts and obstacle configurations. Each planner is integrated into a common greedy assignment procedure that processes tasks according to priority and earliest-start ordering. For each task</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">candidate AGVs are evaluated using planned routes from their current positions to the pickup location and subsequently to the delivery location. Assignment decisions minimize a weighted cost combining estimated completion time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">candidate AGVs are evaluated using planned routes from their current positions to the pickup location and subsequently to the delivery location. Assignment decisions minimize a weighted cost combining estimated completion time</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">route energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">route energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and travel distance. Fleet-level performance is assessed using makespan</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and travel distance. Fleet-level performance is assessed using makespan</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">defined as the maximum completion time across AGVs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">defined as the maximum completion time across AGVs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">together with aggregate energy consumption and total travel distance. Energy estimates account for motion-related costs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">together with aggregate energy consumption and total travel distance. Energy estimates account for motion-related costs</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">including acceleration and turning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">including acceleration and turning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">enabling comparisons beyond geometric path length alone. The benchmark results indicate that A* provides consistently competitive performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">enabling comparisons beyond geometric path length alone. The benchmark results indicate that A* provides consistently competitive performance</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">particularly in energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">particularly in energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">whereas PSO achieves favorable makespan--distance trade-offs in selected scenarios. As a future extension toward online path planning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">whereas PSO achieves favorable makespan--distance trade-offs in selected scenarios. As a future extension toward online path planning</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">the A* algorithm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">the A* algorithm</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">which supports real-time path generation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">which supports real-time path generation</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">is integrated with NSGA-II to optimize task assignment. A Pareto-based analysis is then conducted to compare the performance of the conventional A* approach with that of the proposed A*--NSGA-II framework</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">is integrated with NSGA-II to optimize task assignment. A Pareto-based analysis is then conducted to compare the performance of the conventional A* approach with that of the proposed A*--NSGA-II framework</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">highlighting the trade-offs among the selected operational objectives. The findings demonstrate that shorter travel distances or earlier task completion do not necessarily imply lower energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">highlighting the trade-offs among the selected operational objectives. The findings demonstrate that shorter travel distances or earlier task completion do not necessarily imply lower energy consumption</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">highlighting the need to select planning methods according to warehouse geometry</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">highlighting the need to select planning methods according to warehouse geometry</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">fleet workload</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">fleet workload</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="x-default">and operational priorities.</rdf:li>
     <rdf:li xml:lang="en-US">and operational priorities.</rdf:li>
    </rdf:Alt>
   </dc:description>
